        "topic_names": [
          "4.2 VERSATILE NATURE OF CARBON"
        ],
        "answer": "Carbon's ability to form millions of compounds is primarily due to its unique bonding characteristics. First, it exhibits catenation, allowing it to form long chains, branched chains, or ring structures of carbon atoms. These carbon-carbon bonds are strong and stable, a property not found to the same extent in other elements like silicon. Second, carbon is tetravalent, meaning it has a valency of four, enabling it to bond with four other atoms, such as hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, and halogens. Finally, its small atomic size allows the nucleus to hold onto shared electron pairs strongly, resulting in very strong and stable bonds with most other elements. Together, these properties allow for an immense variety of stable molecules.",
